Subject: [PATCH] winepulse: Preserve the DualShock 4 speaker mix format.

The shared Sony speaker predicate includes DualShock 4 endpoints. Activating
one enables Windows Sony audio mode, which consequently overwrites the DS4
mix format with the DualSense four-channel, 48 kHz format.

Spider-Man then opens the DS4 as a DualSense haptic PCM. A USB 054c:09cc
controller exposes stereo S16 playback at 32 kHz, so the direct PCM rejects
that format. The fallback selects a converting plughw PCM, but active-HID
routing repeatedly tries to reopen the original front PCM while plughw
still owns the device. The resulting busy errors prevent audio delivery.

Keep the probed DS4 mix format instead of applying the DualSense-specific
override. A client using GetMixFormat can then open its speaker through the
existing Pulse speaker path. Leave endpoint identities, reconnect handling,
and the DualSense and DualSense Edge format overrides unchanged.

This addresses the format mismatch observed in the DS4 log; the separate
Edge startup hang is not diagnosed by the successful hotplug capture.
---
 dlls/winepulse.drv/pulse.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/winepulse.drv/pulse.c b/dlls/winepulse.drv/pulse.c
--- a/dlls/winepulse.drv/pulse.c
+++ b/dlls/winepulse.drv/pulse.c
@@ -1619,6 +1619,10 @@ static void apply_windows_sony_audio_format(PhysDevice *dev)
     if (!use_windows_sony_controller_names() || !is_dualsense_speaker_sink(dev))
         return;
 
+    /* DS4 USB audio is stereo, not a split DualSense haptic endpoint. */
+    if (is_dualshock_audio_device(dev))
+        return;
+
     /* Windows exposes the DualSense USB audio function as a four-channel
      * endpoint. PipeWire's UCM profile splits out the controller speaker as a
      * mono sink, but games still use this endpoint for channels 3 and 4 of the
